    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "**BUILD SUCCESS** \u2014 all tests green (2/2, including the two new soft-delete acceptance tests).\n\n## Summary\n\nI converted owner deletion from a hard-delete to a soft-delete. The change touches the layers where each concern belongs:\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a persistent `deleted` boolean column with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted(...)`. A primitive `boolean` defaults to `false`, so a newly created owner is not deleted.\n\n**Delete function** \u2014 `DeleteOwner.java`: instead of `ownerRepository.delete(owner)`, it now flags the owner (`owner.setDeleted(true)`) and saves it, retaining the row. The DELETE pipeline still responds `RespondWithNoContent` (204, 2xx) and the transaction governance is unchanged.\n\n**Create-time duplicate/identity checks** \u2014 the two blocking checks now skip soft-deleted owners:\n- `EnsureUniqueIdentity` \u2014 ignores deleted owners when matching identity keys.\n- `EnsureUniqueHousehold` \u2014 ignores deleted owners when deciding if a household is occupied.\n\nSo a normally-blocking duplicate is admitted when the only matching owner has been soft-deleted. (`DetectPossibleDuplicate` needed no change: the re-created owner shares the deleted owner's telephone, so it isn't soft-flagged anyway.)\n\n**Response contract** \u2014 `openapi.yml`: added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema. The generated `OwnerDto` picks it up and MapStruct maps it automatically from the entity, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns `deleted` (`false` when active, `true` after deletion).\n\n**Schema** \u2014 added the `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE NOT NULL` column to all four DB schemas (hsqldb \u2014 used by the tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres). Note HSQLDB required `DEFAULT` before `NOT NULL`.\n\nOne thing worth flagging: the `jdbc` repository profile (not active in tests) has `findBy*` queries that select an explicit column list not including `deleted`, and its `delete(...)` still hard-deletes. I left it untouched since the tests run under `spring-data-jpa`; if you ever run the `jdbc` profile with soft-delete semantics, those queries would need the column added and a soft-delete variant.",
